00:00:00.570 --> 00:00:02.790
Leah: So, what exactly is deployment?

00:00:03.300 --> 00:00:07.790
Deployment is the process of making new and
existing software products that have been

00:00:07.790 --> 00:00:10.680
completed, available and accessible to users.

00:00:11.330 --> 00:00:16.040
Deployment activities include ‘software
releases’, ‘installation’, ‘testing’,

00:00:16.079 --> 00:00:17.489
and ‘performance monitoring’.

00:00:18.690 --> 00:00:23.720
Deployment planning is critical for software
deployment to be successful, it can be

00:00:23.720 --> 00:00:28.810
manual, but can also be automated, which
makes it more efficient and effective.

00:00:29.439 --> 00:00:35.029
For ‘deployment planning’ the project leader would
outline the scope, approach, and delivery method.

00:00:35.699 --> 00:00:38.420
Chris: Let’s have a look at what
‘Deployment planning’ would include:

00:00:39.280 --> 00:00:44.490
Summary of the deployment goals, which would
include the target date, scope of the work,

00:00:44.710 --> 00:00:46.960
version release goal, and the schedule.

00:00:47.980 --> 00:00:53.609
Deployment schedule, created to break down
manageable tasks which are assigned to specific

00:00:53.609 --> 00:00:56.860
team members with planned duration and dates.

00:00:57.840 --> 00:01:02.949
List of the requirements or resources
needed, for a successful deployment such as

00:01:02.950 --> 00:01:07.169
hardware, software, skillsets, staff time etc.

00:01:08.130 --> 00:01:12.880
Leah: Document and mitigate risks,
potential risks are listed with possible

00:01:12.920 --> 00:01:17.490
impact on the deployment and the steps
to mitigate each risk are documented.

00:01:18.040 --> 00:01:20.220
This would include a rollback strategy.

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:26.429
Communication, a plan to outline how team
members will communicate so everyone knows

00:01:26.429 --> 00:01:28.869
their role and can keep track of tasks.

00:01:29.650 --> 00:01:34.700
The plan would outline each step of the
deployment process in detail and each

00:01:34.700 --> 00:01:36.449
of these processes would be tracked.

00:01:37.120 --> 00:01:42.799
A software tool such as a Release management tool
could be used to automate the deployment plan.

00:01:44.070 --> 00:01:47.059
Chris: So, why is deployment planning important?

00:01:48.080 --> 00:01:53.470
Because of the deployment schedule and that the
resources are determined, this helps developers

00:01:53.480 --> 00:01:58.939
to ‘save time’ as all the resources will be in
place before the software product release happens.

00:02:01.139 --> 00:02:05.919
Leah: Following the processes from software
releases to performance monitoring sequentially,

00:02:06.260 --> 00:02:10.810
will ensure timely feedback, meaning that
developers will be able to evaluate the

00:02:10.820 --> 00:02:12.760
‘efficiency’ of the product properly.

00:02:13.230 --> 00:02:17.880
This in turn will ‘increase productivity’ and
add value to the product for the end users.

00:02:17.880 --> 00:02:23.980
Chris: As developers can measure the efficiency
of the product and get feedback from the

00:02:23.980 --> 00:02:29.200
user, this will help to keep in mind, what
a ‘real end user’ wants from the product.

00:02:29.770 --> 00:02:33.980
For example, a feature of the product
that a user would consider important,

00:02:34.139 --> 00:02:36.100
could be included in the next release.

00:02:37.259 --> 00:02:42.950
This would retain existing users and reach out to
new ones therefore increasing the customer base.

00:02:44.450 --> 00:02:48.930
Leah: If the deployment is planned properly,
this will help the team to be clear on at

00:02:48.930 --> 00:02:54.480
what stage of the project they are at, their
current tasks, and what their priorities are.

00:02:55.110 --> 00:03:00.359
It also helps when a new developer is brought into
the team, as processes are documented properly.

00:03:01.289 --> 00:03:07.340
Chris: It is important to document the ‘risks’ of
software deployment, as they could be ‘mitigated’

00:03:07.340 --> 00:03:09.900
with testing before the product is deployed.

00:03:10.580 --> 00:03:15.079
Risks could include a product that
crashes, has bugs, has performance issues,

00:03:15.460 --> 00:03:17.590
and does not add value to the end user.

00:03:18.340 --> 00:03:23.209
The product can be monitored for responsiveness
and performance in the right test environment.

00:03:24.200 --> 00:03:28.099
One important risk that will need to be
considered is if the deployment needs

00:03:28.099 --> 00:03:30.270
to be rolled back to a previous version.

00:03:31.049 --> 00:03:35.260
A good deployment plan would include a
rollback strategy which we will look at

00:03:35.289 --> 00:03:37.500
in more detail later in this section.

